WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
it's great to be joined on the program  today by Shane Dickson Cavanaugh who is  the staff reporter with The Oregonian in  Portland  Shane great to have you on first of all  thanks for your time hey thanks so much  for inviting me on your show today so  you've been working on this story which  involves university students from Saudi  Arabia who have faced criminal charges  in a number of different American states  this has also happened in Canada and  they've effectively disappeared and the  term disappeared can mean a couple of  different things it can be the good type  of disappearance or the bad type of  disappearance I guess depending on on  who exactly you are in the entire thing  how did this even first get on your  radar well we started with just one case  in particular that was a very big story  at the time here in Portland where a  Saudi national a college student here in  Portland was charged in the fatal  hit-and-run of a teenage girl in a sort  of popular part of Southeast Portland  the Saudi government ended up bailing  this individual out of jail posting  $100,000 so he could be out of jail  while he awaited trial and then weeks  before his trial he suddenly disappears  and this is a guy who was on house  arrest had an ankle monitor and  what-have-you and at the time of his  disappearance that was a pretty big deal  that a foreign national essentially  skipped bail or banished just weeks  before his trial fast forward a year in  some months and I had learned while  talking with a law enforcement source  that federal investigators believed that  the Saudi Arabian government actually  helped this individual  escape prosecution and essentially leave  Oregon and the United States and return  to Saudi Arabia and the circumstances of  his escape the details were pretty  remarkable  we learned that federal law enforcement  assert that a black SUV came to this  individuals house and picked him up  drove him out to sand and gravel yard in  East Portland  where he sliced his ankle monitor and  then drove off investigators believe  that the Saudi Arabian government likely  furnished this individual with an  illicit Passport since he didn't have  one and possibly got him out of the  country or North America on a private  plane so learning just about that one  case here in Portland was pretty  remarkable I mean just the details of it  and then we started looking into this a  bit more in Oregon to see could there  possibly be other cases like this and  within a few weeks we found four other  cases here in Oregon that fit the same  type of pattern specifically young men  students who were studying in the u.s.  from Saudi Arabia were on a scholarship  program from the Saudi government get  arrested and accused of very serious  crimes so in the first case we were  talking about vehicular manslaughter  fatal hit-and-run we're also looking at  rape possession of child pornography  felony DUI assaults all of these  individuals ended up disappearing before  their trials or in one case before one  individual completed his jail sentence  so let's just cross a couple of things  off the list if we can the US and Saudi  Arabia don't have an extradition treaty  and there's not even the remote  possibility that that's what's going on  here  well yes it's true that the US and Saudi  Arabia despite being long-standing  allies we consider Saudi Arabia an  important sort of partner in the Middle  East and obviously we trade the billions  with them a year manufacture arms and  send them over there you know they're  one of the world's largest producers of  oil but we do not have an extradition  treaty with Saudi Arabia which means if  a Saudi citizen here in the US is  accused of a serious crime or any crime  ends up leaving the US and return to  Saudi Arabia the Saudi Arabian  government is not going to turn one of  these individuals back over to the u.s.  to face prosecution I mean there's a  number of countries around the world  that the US doesn't share an extradition  treaty with in a number in the Gulf  region  but for an ally and you know the  considered partner as big as Saudi  Arabia it's a little bit unusual that we  don't have an extradition treaty with  them so when we talk about the the sort  of disappearance obviously you've  pointed out the ways in which Saudi  authorities in the United States might  be involved but would this be possible  without some involvement or at least  indifference from American law  enforcement I mean that's a really good  question and it's obviously one we've  been asking since we've started looking  into this matter and at this point in  time it's not really known what role the  US has had if any in facilitating the  departure of some of these students and  you know I was talking about the cases  here in Oregon and I it's worth  mentioning that since finding the five  cases in Oregon we've started looking  around the US and in Canada to see if  this was happening in other places and  so far we've found cases like these in  seven other states and as well as Canada  and so you know it seems to be a pattern  that's been going on for decades we've  have traced a manslaughter case back to  1988 in Ohio with this exact same thing  happened and prosecutors there I talked  to people who involved in this case  thirty years ago and they you know very  directly he stated they believed that  the Saudi Arabian government helped  facilitate this individuals escape but  in terms of what the u.s. involvement  has been that's not really known but I  mean I think the other sort of question  that we're trying to find the answer to  is what has the US government known and  for how long  because it seems very unlikely that  somebody within the FBI the State  Department the Department of Justice  would not have known about a number of  these you know individuals for nationals  accused of serious crimes essentially  vanished vanishing without a trace I'm  guessing the answer to this next  question is sort of obvious because  they are saudis studying in the united  states but are we talking all about rich  families here people with access to  money wealthy people you know that's a  that's a question that gets that's been  asked a lot since we've been reporting  on these cases and you know it wouldn't  be surprising to think that most of  these kids came from sort of  well-connected or affluent families in  Saudi Arabia  however I'll point out that the King  Abdullah scholarship program which is  sent hundreds of thousands of Saudi  students to the u.s. to study in the  last 15 years or so or actually more  about 13 years it's been hundreds of  thousands of people and not all of them  have come from particularly affluent or  privileged backgrounds I'll go back to  the case in Portland that sort of got  this whole investigation for us started  that defendant his name was Abdul Rahman  Nura I came from what is essentially a  middle class or lower middle-class  family in Saudi Arabia he was raised by  a single mother who was a schoolteacher  so that's not that the type of  individual you're thinking about when we  tend to think about you know Saudi  students who are studying in the US so  the scholarship program provides sort of  full tuition and other perks for  citizens from all backgrounds in Saudi  Arabia  what has the Saudi embassy had to say  about this I'm guessing you've spoken to  them or at least tried to yeah so for  the first the first handful of stories  that we had reported on we have  obviously reached out to the Saudi  embassy in Washington and the Saudi  consulate in Los Angeles was the entity  that had provided bail money for many of  the defendants here in Oregon so we  tried reaching out to individuals at the  consulate they're never heard anything  from them until two US senators both  from Oregon Ron Wayne and Jeff Merkley  introduced legislation in Congress as  sort of a direct response to the  findings in our reporting essentially  the you know the bills would seek to  punish Saudi Arabia if it was found that  they  that the government itself had portrayed  a role in helping some of these  individuals escape prosecution at that  point we started hearing from the Saudi  embassy in Washington and have heard  from them a couple of times since then  the gist of what we've been told and  it's mostly been pretty short structured  statements is that they have said that  the Saudi embassy as a policy will  provide bail money and legal services to  any citizen accused of crimes in the  United States who asked for help but  have categorically denied that they've  played any role whatsoever in  facilitating the departure of  individuals from the u.s. back to Saudi  Arabia now as soon as you're talking  about private planes unless these are  planes that are I don't know contracted  by the parents of these students doesn't  that directly imply that the Saudi  government is doing more than just bail  money and legal services I mean I think  that's a question best asked to federal  law enforcement investigators who sort  of are working with that theory right  now I think the more important thing to  kind of point out with the case of nura  who again was the Saudi national here in  Portland who killed a 15 year old girl  and a number of other cases here in  Oregon and then in other parts of the US  and in Canada a number of these students  as a condition for the release from jail  and they were bailed out were required  to turn their passports over to  authorities now that could have been the  Department of Homeland Security or the  for administrative officer in a  particular jurisdiction or other  authorities so a number of these  students have managed to leave the  United States and return to Saudi Arabia  without a passport  so the question there is how are they  able to travel when their travel  documents have been seized and I think  that's the you know I think that's the  particular detail more so than the  private plane or the prospect of a  private plane that really raises the  question with how the heck are these  guys getting out of the country if they  don't have a passport so you've laid out  what what the the initial sort of pieces  or steps in your investigation has been  in  you've talked to what's going on now and  what sort of your next step or focus in  all of this absolutely so this has  certainly gotten the attention of  senators Wyden and Merkley who have been  raising sort of a stink in Washington  the last couple of weeks or more more so  the last couple of months obviously  they've introduced bills into Congress  senator Wyden during who sits on the  Select Committee on Intelligence in the  Senate asked Christopher Rea the FBI  director and an open committee hearing a  month ago what the FBI knew about these  types of cases and asked if the FBI  could report back to him about any  involvement the Saudi government  government may have had in facilitating  the departure of these criminal  defendants or other ones senator Merkley  yesterday who's on the Foreign Relations  Committee was sort of asking the Trump  Trump nominee for ambassador to Saudi  Arabia about these cases and after weeks  of sort of raising questions it sounds  like a federal investigation or a kind  of probe is underway where a number of  agencies including Customs and Border  Protection the Department of Justice and  possibly the State Department are now  looking into these disappearances or at  least have told the you know senators  Wyden and Merkley that they're doing so  so the last thing I wanted to touch on  was you've looked at Saudi nationals and  and sort of what has happened there is  there any other country where there has  been a sort of similar disappearance of  people charged with crimes where it's  possible that the government was  involved in some way that's a really  great question and it's an important one  and it's one I think we hope to seek to  answer a little later down the line  certainly we've been asked the question  over and over again you know why are you  focusing sort of exclusively on Saudi  Arabia at this time and it's simply  because the first case we dealt with  and we thought we were dealing with just  one unusual incident here in Portland  involved a Saudi national and then once  we found that there were five here in  Oregon we decided we're gonna start  looking around the country and that's  something we're still doing now is  looking for other cases and we're  actually finding them and finding some  and other interesting things along the  way but you know clearly this hasn't  been sort of an attempt to single out  Saudi Arabia in particular but since  they are a close ally of the United  States because they don't have an  extradition treaty with us you know  we've been very intent on looking at  this you know particular country and  also because there's been a number of  questions sort of raised with in recent  months about you know the us-saudi  relations in the aftermath of the  killing of journalist Jamal khashoggi  the war in Yemen and other sort of  conduct by the Saudi Arabian government  abroad so that sort of fits into that  larger context as well but going back to  your question obviously we want to sort  of figure out who else you know from  from other parts of the world might be  participating in in similar ventures  really excellent work we've been  speaking with Shane Dickson Cavanaugh  who is a staff reporter with The  Oregonian in Portland we're going to  keep following the story Shane and I  appreciate you sharing it with our  audience Dave thanks so much for having  me on today

